{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3284","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3284","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3284\",\"parent_revision\":\"814564ee55ad54ccf690300ac1f47e10c98adfe794475272ceeb44e482e53fb0\",\"root_revision\":\"814564ee55ad54ccf690300ac1f47e10c98adfe794475272ceeb44e482e53fb0\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-28\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-28/29 while running `/dh:work-backlog-item work --auto #3268`. `implement-feature/SKILL.md`'s \\\"Commit Ownership\\\" section states, for `full_auto`/`checkpoint` autonomy: \\\"Do NOT commit after each individual step 4b \u2014 other batch agents may still be writing to the worktree. Commit once after step 5 confirms all tasks in the current batch are complete.\\\" The orchestrator is meant to own exactly one batch commit per wave.\\n\\nPlan Pb31107bf ran under `autonomy: full_auto`. Its first wave (T1, T3, T4, T5) was dispatched in parallel, all four editing disjoint files. Each task's own `verification-steps` (authored by `swarm-task-planner`, not by the orchestrator) explicitly instructed the dispatched `dh:task-worker` agent to run `git add <its file>` then `git commit -m \\\"...\\\"` itself as the task's own final step \u2014 e.g. T1's step 5: \\\"Run `git add plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` then `git commit -m ...`\\\". All four tasks committed individually and concurrently in the same shared worktree (commits e5aa9b85a, 620c6fcc6, ee23bb4b7, 8962e1589), rather than the orchestrator collecting the wave and issuing one batch commit after step 5.\\n\\nVerified no actual corruption occurred this run: each commit's `git show --stat` is scoped to exactly its own task's target file(s) (T5's commit additionally touched 3 `plugin.json` files, confirmed as the repo's pre-commit manifest-sync hook firing, not cross-task contamination). The four `git commit` calls happened to serialize safely through git's own commit-lock. But `implement-feature/SKILL.md`'s explicit warning (\\\"other batch agents may still be writing to the worktree\\\") describes exactly the race this pattern invites, and nothing currently prevents it \u2014 this run avoided the race by luck of timing, not by design.\\n\\nRoot cause not diagnosed: it's unclear whether `swarm-task-planner`'s task-template generation logic always appends a per-task commit instruction regardless of `autonomy` mode, or whether this is specific to how it built this plan's `verification-steps`. Same defect shape as #3277 and #3278 \u2014 two independently-authored parts of the SAM pipeline (a plan-generating agent and an execution-loop skill) assert contradictory ownership of the same operation, and nothing enforces the documented one.\\n\\nUser-provided context: none \u2014 whether the fix is removing per-task commit instructions from `swarm-task-planner`'s generated verification-steps, changing `implement-feature`'s rule to explicitly allow per-task commits when tasks are file-disjoint, or something else is a design question for grooming.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 development-harness /dh:work-backlog-item work --auto #3268, plan Pb31107bf\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"3e44a7e5af4ce90f83939ca11b8458c76b68cfac1bda5be6022abe6b8e14fc57\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRSB1NQ\"}"}